In quantum chromodynamics, hadrons are color-singlet bound states of quarks and gluons. Beyond ordinary mesons (quark–antiquark) and baryons (three quarks), experiments have established multiquark resonances with valence content of four quarks (tetraquarks, e.g. with content qq q̄ q̄) and five quarks (pentaquarks, e.g. with content qqqq q̄). Three competing pictures for the internal structure of such exotic resonances are commonly considered:

(i) Compact ("bag-like") states: all four or five (anti)quarks are confined within a single color-singlet region and are bound by direct color forces, with diquark/antidiquark or other color-correlated substructures held tightly together;

(ii) Hadronic molecules: two color-singlet hadrons (a meson–meson pair for tetraquarks, or a meson–baryon pair for pentaquarks) are loosely bound by residual hadronic forces (analogous to nuclear binding), with masses lying close to the relevant two-hadron threshold;

(iii) Colored-molecule states (proposed for certain exotics containing two heavy quarks, such as doubly-heavy tetraquarks): two coloured substructures (e.g. a heavy-heavy diquark and a light antiquark-antiquark pair, or two colour-octet clusters) bound by long-range chromodynamic forces, intermediate between (i) and (ii).

Problem. Determine, for each experimentally observed exotic tetraquark and pentaquark resonance, which of the three internal-structure pictures (compact, hadronic-molecule, or colored-molecule) correctly describes it, including the possibility that the physical state is a quantum superposition of these configurations. Provide a QCD-based criterion that, given the quantum numbers, mass, width, production rates, and decay patterns of such a resonance, unambiguously fixes its internal structure.

